{
  "case_id": "2006-05-547",
  "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner Lopez",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LONG: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: A drug-trafficking crime is defined in 18 U.S. Code Section 924(c) as any felony punishable under the Controlled Substances Act or two other Federal statutes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, do you agree that if there were a State conviction for an offense that would have been punishable under the Federal law, their both being felonies, that enhancement can apply, in that instance, and that that does meet the definition?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Where are those sections?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: In 8 U.S.C. 1101(a)(43)(B), what, in your view, does the first phrase, \"illicit trafficking in a controlled substance,\" add to the reference to a drug-trafficking crime --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Crooks",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CROOKS: We cited, in our reply brief at page 4, Your Honor, the Hamdi case, United States v. Hamdi, from the Second Circuit, where the Second Circuit found that the possibility of reducing the term of imprisonment, even retrospectively, could have an impact on the ability to get a waiver of inadmissibility in --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Crooks",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CROOKS: But this Court has never held that a -- it would be unfair to hold that an appeal of a sentence can be moot while that sentence is still in effect, and when it can still have consequences for Mr. Toledo-Flores."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Is there any indication that that's in the works, or is this just another conceivable thing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Crooks",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I wanted to point out that, in Campo Serrano, which we've cited in our brief, this Court held that a deported alien's probation term was a continuing criminal sentence that saved the case from mootness. And the Court pointed to the fact that he -- that the deported alien in Campo Serrano could, just like Mr. Toledo-Flores, have his conditional release revoked, and he could be reimprisoned, upon a finding that he had violated his conditions. And we believe that Campo Serrano points very strongly in the direction that this case is not moot."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kneedler",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KNEEDLER: It expressly incorporates it, yes. Yes. But the -- but the operative provision is section 1101(a)(43) of the INA, and there are three features of that that we think reinforce this conclusion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kneedler",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KNEEDLER: Our point is not that 924(c) requires a conviction when it's in the Immigration Act, it's that the immigration consequences of that incorporation turn on whether someone was convicted of an aggravated felony."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: And is it ever true that a State felony is punishable under a Federal statute?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Kneedler, could I ask you about your assertion that you \"look to the State of conviction\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, I thought -- I thought you conceded that a State offense could constitute illicit trafficking --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: You think there's a uniform Federal definition of a theft offense?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e3765dd5a716713bc161ee2666a85786ef7809d8ce4dbc68a524e2779d3fd43c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e3765dd5a716-0003-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "aggravated felony, you may not become a U.S. citizen. You are foreclosed from establishing good moral character. So, in fact, citizenship is at stake here, and the Uniformity Clause is in play."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-05-547",
